Mohamed el-erian is an influential voice  in the financial world  he led PIMCO for years running the  world's largest bond fund now he's the  chief economic adviser at Allianz and he  says the economy could look very  different as we emerge from the pandemic  I sat down with him on Friday  mr. el-erian new unemployment numbers  came in this week just under 3 million  and White House economic advisor Kevin  Hassett said while that is a tough  number it's an indication that the  economy is starting to come back here he  is I think the fact that we came in  under 3 million suggests that the  turning on of the economy is beginning  and it's beginning to show up with the  data so what we expect now is that  claims will continue to decline as the  economy turns back do you see the jobs  picture getting brighter right now so it  will get brighter in the sense that will  get bad at a slower rate but we should  not forget how bad this picture is with  the additional 3 million Chris that is  36 million people have signed up for on  topless claims for in 8 weeks that's one  quarter of our labor force so it's an  enormous shock so the consistent  forecasts from the White House is of a  sharp steep quick downturn to be  followed in a v-shape by a sharp quick  rebound here is President Trump or not  this week we're gonna do well in the  fourth quarter and I think next year  with all of the stimulus orders and  things we've done I think we're gonna  have one of the best economic years  we've ever had  do you see a v-shaped recovery as the  White House is predicting that the  economy is gonna come back as quickly  and as sharply as it went down I sure  hope so but I think it's uncertain and  it's uncertain because both the journey  to next year and what next year look  like are uncertain  so rather than think of letters think of  a pendulum Chris we were operating in a  normal world as you and I defined it  we then swung all the way on the other  side to a lockdown and now we're gonna  swing back and forth trying to find  where the new equilibrium is so it's not  gonna be a neat Vee it's gonna be more  like a series of w's if you like but  think of a pendulum swinging and we  don't know the magnitude of the swings  and we don't know the duration of the  swing or the settling point so it really  is an uncertain outlook I want to pick  up on that because you say people are  being naive about how easy it and quick  it is gonna be to reopen the economy  that we have to deal with health factors  and economic factors and even behavioral  factors explain what you mean both in  terms of this country and globally in  reopening the economy so this is the  biggest shock we've had for generations  and it weighs it's we issue one is the  main reason for the shock is health and  there's gonna be a long time until you  and I trust each other  it's what Eric Schmidt calls the  information failure it's very difficult  for me to convince you I'm healthy and  for you to convince me I'm healthy till  we get a vaccine so the health issue  doesn't get resolved overnight secondly  you have shaken consumer confidence you  have shaken behaviors our economy isn't  wired for social distancing so we're  gonna have to discover how to do that  and third businesses it's not clear how  you we start a business when different  states are doing different things and  when different countries are going to do  different things so we have these three  massive uncertainties all in play at the  same time and it's very hard to say oh  everything's gonna be resolved overnight  we hope so but I think we should buckle  our seat belt that is gonna get bumpy  still well there's another uncertainty  and debate and that is over what to do  in Washington next Democrats won a three  trillion dollar another huge stimulus  bill Republicans say let's take a pause  and see how the money siphons through  the  economy that's been approved so far and  this week you had Federal Reserve  Chairman Jerome Powell say that this has  been a real economic shock and we do  need more stimulus here he is on that  well the economic response has been both  timely and appropriately large it may  not be the final chapter given that the  path ahead is both highly uncertain and  subject to significant downside risks do  you think we need more government  stimulus and if so how much and how  quickly  so so far Chris we haven't had  government stimulus we've had government  relief and it's a good thing we've had  it and it's a good thing that Washington  reacted five trillion dollars worth of  reaction because despite that this week  also gave us not just a horrible  unemployment number but the worst  industrial output collapse in a century  and the worst retail sale collapse on  record so so we are hurting and thank  God that Washington has reacted on the  relief measures what's important now is  not to lose sight that there's a second  phase this is not just about winning the  war against a global depression and a  u.s. depression this is about securing  the peace about making sure that we  emerge from this also strong so the next  stage has to also focus on key issues  like infrastructure labor retailing and  retooling safety nets things that are  going to be critical if we are to bounce  back in a sustainable manner otherwise  we're gonna get stuck in a new normal of  really mediocre growth and greater  social tensions and then there are the  financial markets which I think have  surprised a lot of people by remaining  surprisingly strong in fact the markets  are up about 25 percent from their  bottom in late March you say that the  financial markets are in one universe  and the real economy is in a different  universe what do you mean they're  looking at different things so you and I  and the real economy are wondering when  do we get to go back to  when other stores open again we feel  very big disruptions long everyday life  the marketplace are saying look that's  fine but that's all short-term we look  to the long-term and when they look to  the long-term they see a win-win  hypothesis I think they're too  optimistic but let me explain what they  see first a bet that we're going to come  back quickly the V and if we come back  quickly then looking through which is  the phrase our hero most this awful  period is the right thing to do but they  have a second win Chris if we get that  wrong it doesn't matter because the  Federal Reserve is buying assets and is  buying assets with that we never  imagined it could buy so there's this  sense of comfort that you get to win  whatever the outlook I think Chris that  it's too optimistic but markets do what  they do well I was gonna ask you who do  you think the markets as one of the  great investors do you think the markets  are gonna say stay as strong as they are  right now or do you think at some point  the two universes are gonna collide and  reality will intrude so most of us who  think fundamentals are important that  ultimately you are betting on company's  profitability and you're betting on the  viability of a system or courses here  and we've had several warnings from  really respected investors but I also  understand those who say that's fine but  you are dinosaurs we are betting on  something different we are betting on  the Federal Reserve and if you have a  printing press in the basement then you  can have a massive influence on markets  I personally think it's easier to make a  relative call between different  companies  I get nervous betting on the overall  level of the market right here Mohamed  el-erian thank you so much  it is always fascinating to talk to you  and to get a little dose of reality  thanks so much sir thank you Chris
 

